`,s.jsxs(i.p,{children:["Right away I recognized some similar concepts from JavaScript, but they look a little different with Kotlin. For example, the Elvis operator ",s.jsx(i.code,{children:"(?:)"})," basically works like the ternary operator, which makes null-safety a lot cleaner. Classes in Kotlin automatically come with hidden getters and setters, and when it comes to variables, ",s.jsx(i.code,{children:"var"})," makes them mutable while ",s.jsx(i.code,{children:"val"})," locks them down as immutable (const). Inheritance also works differently; if you want a class or a function to be extended or overridden, you have to explicitly mark it with the ",s.jsx(i.code,{children:"open"})," keyword. Another thing I noticed is how Kotlin treats functions as values. Instead of just passing the name, you need to use the function reference operator (",s.jsx(i.code,{children:"::"}),"). And finally, trailing lambda functions are… interesting!"]}),`
`,s.jsx(i.p,{children:"I also reviewed how the four core pillars of OOP translate into Kotlin:"}),`
`,s.jsxs(i.ul,{children:[`
`,s.jsxs(i.li,{children:["Encapsulation is handled through visibility modifiers, just like in other languages, using ",s.jsx(i.code,{children:"public"}),", ",s.jsx(i.code,{children:"private"}),", ",s.jsx(i.code,{children:"protected"}),", and ",s.jsx(i.code,{children:"internal"})," keywords."]}),`
`,s.jsx(i.li,{children:"Polymorphism shows up when overriding functions and variables."}),`
`,s.jsxs(i.li,{children:["Inheritance works with ",s.jsx(i.code,{children:"extends"})," but again only if the parent class is marked ",s.jsx(i.code,{children:"open"}),"."]}),`
`,s.jsxs(i.li,{children:["Abstraction comes into play with interfaces, which let you define ",s.jsx(i.em,{children:"what"})," needs to be done without specifying down ",s.jsx(i.em,{children:"how"})," it’s implemented."]}),`
`]}),`
